WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.510
Aegis is a runtime trust layer for AI agents, built with Moss.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.383
An assistant that refuses everything is not reliable.

00:00:06.383 --> 00:00:10.990
Aegis makes both protection and usefulness visible, with evidence for every decision.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.350
This walkthrough uses a fictional bank and synthetic policies.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.568
Start with an ordinary policy question.

00:00:17.568 --> 00:00:21.288
Moss retrieves relevant sources using local semantic search.

00:00:21.288 --> 00:00:24.575
Aegis verifies their hashes before calling the model.

00:00:24.575 --> 00:00:29.597
The candidate then passes privacy, numerical evidence, and source overlap checks.

00:00:29.597 --> 00:00:33.317
The sources show exactly where the transfer limit came from.

00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:37.905
Native search, embedding, and total response time are measured separately.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:44.918
Now an attacker asks the assistant to ignore its instructions and expose its system prompt.

00:00:44.918 --> 00:00:46.700
The input gate blocks the request.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.782
Downstream stages are skipped, and the decision receipt records that generation was never called.

00:00:51.782 --> 00:00:54.350
The protection is visible in the execution trace.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:01:00.074
The next test assumes the user is innocent, but retrieved context has been tampered with.

00:01:00.074 --> 00:01:04.999
This labeled simulation inserts a hostile passage using a legitimate document identifier.

00:01:04.999 --> 00:01:08.651
Aegis checks the actual content against its trusted hash manifest.

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:11.694
The mismatch quarantines the context before generation.

00:01:11.694 --> 00:01:14.350
A high retrieval rank cannot override this gate.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.238
A topically similar answer can still invent a policy.

00:01:18.238 --> 00:01:22.607
This controlled test injects an unsupported amount into a candidate answer.

00:01:22.607 --> 00:01:26.976
The numerical evidence check catches the claim and withholds the candidate.

00:01:26.976 --> 00:01:30.413
The rejected text is not returned or retained in the trace.

00:01:30.413 --> 00:01:33.851
Similarity is a signal, not proof that every claim is true.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.304
When retrieval is unavailable, Aegis declines to guess.

00:01:38.304 --> 00:01:42.777
It labels the turn unavailable rather than counting it as a successful answer.

00:01:42.777 --> 00:01:45.759
This outage simulation affects only its own request.

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:52.411
A new refund question can use the normal pipeline and recover, without a global switch that disrupts other visitors.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:02:00.238
The receipt identifies the actual provider, model, attempts, and reported token use.

00:02:00.238 --> 00:02:02.358
Export it to preserve the evidence.

00:02:02.358 --> 00:02:09.383
Moss also powers semantic search over this visitor's redacted session traces, helping find earlier blocked requests.

00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:14.350
This is temporary session memory; it is not presented as a durable audit database.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:20.166
The evaluation lab runs thirty-two public regression cases through the real pipeline.

00:02:20.166 --> 00:02:23.884
Twenty are adversarial; twelve are legitimate policy questions.

00:02:23.884 --> 00:02:30.729
A refused customer question, missing evidence, failed provider, or missed latency budget cannot become a green pass.

00:02:30.729 --> 00:02:35.450
The run reports safety and usefulness separately, and every case is inspectable.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:40.289
These are observed results from a development suite, not a security certification.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.091
The same suite was run once per generator with fallback disabled.

00:02:49.091 --> 00:02:52.426
Groq passed thirty-two cases; Gemini passed thirty-one.

00:02:52.426 --> 00:02:56.064
The unavailable Gemini request remains visible as a failure.

00:02:56.064 --> 00:03:00.430
Groq is primary, with Gemini available for one bounded recovery attempt.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:08.495
A separate fault-injection receipt records a real Gemini answer after a simulated primary error, still checked by every release gate.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:15.159
Moss powers threat retrieval, policy retrieval, answer-to-source verification, and trace search.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:19.176
A bundled encoder removes runtime model downloads, without a vector database.

00:03:19.176 --> 00:03:23.350
The architecture, product requirements, source, and measured reports are public.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:29.350
Built by Syed Maaz, Aegis makes agent reliability inspectable, testable, and useful, with its limits stated openly.
